WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.216 --> 00:00:03.049
(penguin honking)

00:00:12.848 --> 00:00:16.181
(chill, rhythmic music)

00:00:21.978 --> 00:00:22.811
- Guys, it's--

00:00:22.811 --> 00:00:23.845
- [Jen] 7:50!

00:00:23.845 --> 00:00:24.899
- 7:50.

00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:27.549
The only reason we would
ever wake up this early

00:00:27.549 --> 00:00:28.629
is because we're going to--

00:00:28.629 --> 00:00:29.789
- [Jen] The Shedd Aquarium!

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:30.909
- [Steven] The Shedd Aquarium!

00:00:30.909 --> 00:00:32.770
- It's probably the nicest aquarium

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.400
in the Midwest, maybe America.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.258
It's gigantic, it's awesome,
it's very expansive.

00:00:37.258 --> 00:00:40.309
- And we're gonna get a
behind-the-scenes, sneak peek look

00:00:40.309 --> 00:00:42.559
at otters, penguins, trees.

00:00:43.572 --> 00:00:44.405
- [Jen] What?

00:00:44.405 --> 00:00:45.346
- Bats!

00:00:45.346 --> 00:00:46.179
- [Jen] Bats?

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:48.829
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:51.331
- My name is Michael Pratt,
I am a trainer up at the

00:00:51.331 --> 00:00:52.611
marine mammal department,
and you guys are here

00:00:52.611 --> 00:00:54.531
to experience our Trainer for a Day.

00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:56.891
- So people can actually
have this experience as well?

00:00:56.891 --> 00:00:58.709
- Absolutely, they throw
on a pair of rubber boots,

00:00:58.709 --> 00:01:00.779
just like you guys got on, and
they follow a trainer around

00:01:00.779 --> 00:01:02.379
for about four hours, and
they get to experience

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:04.131
the highlights of what we do every day.

00:01:04.131 --> 00:01:06.440
We also wanna inspire people
to work towards conservation,

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:07.989
protecting these animals out in the wild.

00:01:07.989 --> 00:01:09.443
- Alright, let's do this!
- Yeah!

00:01:09.443 --> 00:01:10.493
- Let's just get right to it.

00:01:10.493 --> 00:01:12.537
(chill music)

00:01:12.537 --> 00:01:14.017
- So this is the marine mammal kitchen.

00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:16.240
It's pretty much the busiest
place in our department.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.949
And we usually hand-sort
through all the food.

00:01:17.949 --> 00:01:19.336
Right now these guys are sorting through

00:01:19.336 --> 00:01:20.771
some clam for our sea otters.

00:01:20.771 --> 00:01:22.571
It's also really important for us to find

00:01:22.571 --> 00:01:24.329
sustainable seafood to feed our animals.

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:27.219
We go through about 800 pounds
of restaurant-quality food

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.210
to feed all of our
animals every single day.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.124
So we are gonna be
sorting through this clam,

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:34.099
you can grab a piece of
clam, pull of this top part,

00:01:34.099 --> 00:01:35.799
and this is the foot, or the stomach,

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:37.840
and this is what most of
their diet is made up of.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.971
It actually costs the same
amount of money to feed

00:01:39.971 --> 00:01:42.840
an adult sea otter as it does
to feed an adult beluga whale.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.662
- Oh my god.
- Oh my god.

00:01:44.662 --> 00:01:45.495
That's quite a size difference.

00:01:45.495 --> 00:01:47.359
- That's a 40-60 pound animal, versus a

00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:49.480
potentially close to 2,000 pound animal.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:50.691
- This is how kings eat, though.

00:01:50.691 --> 00:01:53.963
- Yeah, the animals have poison testers.

00:01:53.963 --> 00:01:56.901
All the traitors that eat
it first, just to make sure.

00:01:56.901 --> 00:01:59.901
(upbeat jazz music)

00:02:05.223 --> 00:02:06.564
- [Jen] They're just looking
at us like who the hell

00:02:06.564 --> 00:02:07.985
are you losers?

00:02:07.985 --> 00:02:08.955
Holy crap.

00:02:08.955 --> 00:02:10.741
- [Steven] This one loves you, Jen!

00:02:10.741 --> 00:02:12.876
- [Jen] Probably just touched
poop, I don't even care.

00:02:12.876 --> 00:02:14.716
- [Steven] Wanna see yourself?

00:02:14.716 --> 00:02:17.549
(penguin honking)

00:02:19.276 --> 00:02:21.339
- So the vocalizing, cause
there's a bunch of new people

00:02:21.339 --> 00:02:24.665
out here, they want to
let themselves be known.

00:02:24.665 --> 00:02:26.974
They actually used penguin vocalizations

00:02:26.974 --> 00:02:29.404
to create some of the dinosaur
noises in Jurassic Park.

00:02:29.404 --> 00:02:31.236
Cookie's a very important
penguin here at Shedd.

00:02:31.236 --> 00:02:33.404
She will be turning 30
years old this year.

00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:36.956
Cookie actually recently
had cataract surgery.

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:37.956
- Oh my god.

00:02:39.694 --> 00:02:41.396
So do you hang out with
these penguins every day?

00:02:41.396 --> 00:02:43.924
- I've been working with them
for about seven and 1/2 years,

00:02:43.924 --> 00:02:46.886
so I know them really well,
they know me really well,

00:02:46.886 --> 00:02:47.995
we've got a good relationship.

00:02:47.995 --> 00:02:50.383
(upbeat music)

00:02:50.383 --> 00:02:51.392
So one of the really important things

00:02:51.392 --> 00:02:54.233
for animals here at Shedd
is environmental enrichment.

00:02:54.233 --> 00:02:55.222
Providing changes to their environment

00:02:55.222 --> 00:02:56.454
to keep them stimulated.

00:02:56.454 --> 00:02:58.934
These are some environmental
enrichment devices,

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:00.453
also known as toys.

00:03:00.453 --> 00:03:04.366
Just grab a couple of pieces
of food, stick them in there.

00:03:04.366 --> 00:03:06.289
So what we're gonna do
is give these to Ellie,

00:03:06.289 --> 00:03:07.748
our youngest otter.

00:03:07.748 --> 00:03:08.773
- [Steven] Oh, hi!

00:03:08.773 --> 00:03:10.028
[Jen] Oh my god.

00:03:10.028 --> 00:03:10.911
- [Steven] You want this, Ellie?

00:03:10.911 --> 00:03:11.911
Here you go.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:14.324
Oh, wow!

00:03:14.324 --> 00:03:16.280
- So it keeps their minds active,

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.598
they gotta work to figure it out,

00:03:17.598 --> 00:03:18.968
it's not just given to them.

00:03:18.968 --> 00:03:20.300
Some of the otters it takes a little while

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.388
for them to learn which way to push it,

00:03:21.388 --> 00:03:23.409
but she knows what to do.

00:03:23.409 --> 00:03:24.916
- [Jen] Where do these otters come from?

00:03:24.916 --> 00:03:26.206
What are their stories?

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:27.977
- Here at Shedd we have five sea otters,

00:03:27.977 --> 00:03:30.857
and four of them were directly
rescued from the wild.

00:03:30.857 --> 00:03:33.846
They were orphaned, or somehow
separated from their mother.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:35.004
So one of the nice
things about working with

00:03:35.004 --> 00:03:36.777
the animals here at Shedd,
is it gives us the skills

00:03:36.777 --> 00:03:38.766
that we can use to help
animals out in the wild.

00:03:38.766 --> 00:03:41.057
This is Luna and Mari.

00:03:41.057 --> 00:03:41.903
- [Steven] Oh my gosh!

00:03:41.903 --> 00:03:44.617
- I would just hang out here
all day if I worked here.

00:03:44.617 --> 00:03:46.296
I'd be like, I'm otter girl.

00:03:46.296 --> 00:03:49.016
- Sea otters actually have
the densest fur of any mammal,

00:03:49.016 --> 00:03:50.397
because unlike most marine mammals,

00:03:50.397 --> 00:03:52.635
they don't have a thick layer
of blubber to keep them warm,

00:03:52.635 --> 00:03:54.256
so they're relying on their coat.

00:03:54.256 --> 00:03:55.208
It's also one of the reasons that they eat

00:03:55.208 --> 00:03:57.896
so much food, too, is because
their metabolism runs so fast

00:03:57.896 --> 00:03:59.768
to keep up a high body temperature.

00:03:59.768 --> 00:04:01.368
These are some of the treats
that we give the otters,

00:04:01.368 --> 00:04:02.826
it's just clam mixed up with water.

00:04:02.826 --> 00:04:04.728
We put it in some little
molds and freeze it.

00:04:04.728 --> 00:04:06.275
- [Jen] Oh, they knew,
she was like, I'm here.

00:04:06.275 --> 00:04:07.368
- [Steven] Hi!

00:04:07.368 --> 00:04:08.428
- Just toss it in there,

00:04:08.428 --> 00:04:09.848
just don't get your hands too close.

00:04:09.848 --> 00:04:11.765
- [Steven] Here you go.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.549
- They will start to stash
them in a little pocket

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:18.778
that they have under their forelimbs.

00:04:18.778 --> 00:04:20.069
They're really intelligent animals,

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:21.778
they just have a really
short attention span.

00:04:21.778 --> 00:04:22.949
They get bored really easily.

00:04:22.949 --> 00:04:23.818
- [Jen] Fair enough.

00:04:23.818 --> 00:04:26.568
- Highly intelligent, low attention span.

00:04:26.568 --> 00:04:30.735
I'm the beluga whale, feed
me and I'll do anything.

00:04:32.648 --> 00:04:34.928
- It was absolutely
phenomenal, it was like

00:04:34.928 --> 00:04:37.298
the coolest (bleep)ing we could've done.

00:04:37.298 --> 00:04:39.007
- [Steven] One thing you just
don't realize is how much

00:04:39.007 --> 00:04:42.127
these people love the
animals that they work with.

00:04:42.127 --> 00:04:44.880
- They did tell us a lot about
their conservation efforts,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.098
and how they try to use their research

00:04:47.098 --> 00:04:50.688
specifically to foster a better
rescue and rehabilitation

00:04:50.688 --> 00:04:53.480
of orphaned animals or
animals that are sick.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.368
I wish we got to touch the otters,

00:04:55.368 --> 00:04:58.894
but I understand that they
are fragile, gentle creatures,

00:04:58.894 --> 00:05:00.935
and sometimes you just
look, you don't touch.

00:05:00.935 --> 00:05:03.435
(chill music)

00:05:13.288 --> 00:05:14.500
(sign squeaking)

